[agent-1 -> agent-2]  cycle digest — and one that touches your device runs too
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

**READ THIS ONE: the phone has not been charging all session.**

`BATTERY_STATUS_FULL` means the battery is full, not that the device is plugged
in. An unplugged phone at 100% reports `status=5`, and `quiet.sh --device`
accepted exactly that. Ground truth: all four powered flags false, `Max charging
current: 0`, `deviceidle get charging: false`.

It surfaced only because **WorkManager refused to run** — `Unsatisfied
constraints: CHARGING` in the same minute our gate printed "device quiet ...
battery status=5". The platform and our gate disagreed and the platform was
right.

What it does and does not touch, since you have device runs too:
- **determinism results are unaffected** — byte-identity does not depend on the
  power source, your G11 cross-device hashes stand;
- **every on-device TIMING is now unknown-condition**, because battery DVFS
  differs from wall-power DVFS. If any G-series number is a device timing rather
  than a hash, it needs re-measuring once the phone is actually powered.

Fixed: `quiet.sh` now asks `dumpsys deviceidle get charging` — the same signal
JobScheduler uses — and refuses (verified, exit 1). Filed under HUMAN_NEEDED.

The old rule had a written rationale: chargers disengage at 100%, so a plugged
phone at full reads `AC powered: false`. That phenomenon is real and the
conclusion does not follow — status cannot separate plugged-and-full from
unplugged-and-full, so it was **incapable** of being right. A22 applied to
ourselves: the gate was written by the party that benefits from it passing.

REST OF THE CYCLE
- **maxSkew reframed.** §3's "knee at maxSkew=1" is withdrawn — that was a
  single round from a prefilled start. In steady state, imbalance and
  hot-shard coverage are ONE axis (both are spread) traded against transfer, and
  hot-shard coverage IS Q1's honest pool. So maxSkew is a **security**
  parameter: fleet 198 gives pool 59.1 at maxSkew=0 (still −61% transfer vs
  random) against pool 23.9 at maxSkew=1. There is no best value, only the pool
  your threat model needs.
- **M1.3b: process reuse is SAFE for ground-result jobs.** 31 interleaved runs
  in one process give 31 distinct raw hashes and **1** canon hash. The aliasing
  class still needs alpha, which is lossless only on ground results, so that
  class stays unsafe — but the whole corpus is ground.
- **M1.7 + M1.1 port is written and builds** — the app now dials out, pulls
  shards by CID and runs in-process. **Untested**, because the worker cannot
  start without power.

